          "turn_index": 0,
          "type": "turn",
          "question": "My HbA1c came back at 7.2%. What does that mean, what target does guidance describe, and which code would type 2 diabetes be recorded under?",
          "answer": "An HbA1c level of 7.2% indicates that your blood sugar levels have been higher than the normal range over the past two to three months. Generally, guidance recommends that an HbA1c below 7% is a reasonable target for many non-pregnant adults with diabetes, as this level helps reduce complications associated with diabetes. However, goals can vary based on individual circumstances.\n\nType 2 diabetes is recorded under code E11 in the ICD-10 classification. This code is part of Chapter 4, which addresses endocrine, nutritional, and metabolic diseases. It separates diabetes by cause, with type 2 diabetes being one of the categories.\n\nI couldn't cover specifics regarding how your individual result might be interpreted in the context of your overall health or specific management strategies, as these should be discussed with a healthcare professional.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
